<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Ibbi-Sin</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">r. c. 2028–2004 BCE</span> · <a href="/centuries/21st-century-bce">21st century BCE</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Ibbi-Sin was the last king of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/third-dynasty-of-ur">Third Dynasty of Ur</a>, reigning about 2028–2004 BCE. His reign saw the empire break apart as provinces broke away, Amorite incursions and famine struck, and his official <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ishbi-erra">Ishbi-Erra</a> made himself independent at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/isin">Isin</a>. In about 2004 BCE the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/elamites">Elamites</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shimashki-dynasty">Shimashki</a> kings sacked <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ur">Ur</a> and carried Ibbi-Sin captive to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/anshan">Anshan</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/ibbi-sin.jpg" alt="Ibbi-Sin" width="320" height="367" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Metropolitan Museum of Art · CC0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Ibbi-Sin_enthroned.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The sack of Ur around 2004 BCE, when the Shimashki king Kindattu captured <b>Ibbi-Sin</b>, the last ruler of the Third Dynasty of Ur, and ended that dynasty's domination of Elam, represents the first major offensive achievement.</blockquote><a href="/empire/elamite-confederation">Elamite Confederation · Military</a></li><li><blockquote>After <b>Ibbi-Sin</b>'s fifth year his year names stop at Lagash, a sign that Ur had lost the city.</blockquote><a href="/empire/lagash">Lagash · Governance &amp; Law</a></li><li><blockquote>The highly centralised system, though efficient, proved vulnerable to disruption, particularly as provincial governors asserted their autonomy during the reign of the last king, <b>Ibbi-Sin</b>.</blockquote><a href="/empire/neo-sumerian-empire">Neo-Sumerian Empire · Decline &amp; Fall</a></li><li><blockquote>The Third Dynasty's own end came by arms, when the Elamites took the city and carried off King <b>Ibbi-Sin</b>, the event mourned in the Lament for Ur.</blockquote><a href="/empire/ur">Ur · Military</a></li><li><blockquote>…from the temple library at Nippur in the University of Pennsylvania Museum; George Barton translated them in 1918 as &quot;A prayer for the city of Ur&quot;, noting that it was plausible but unproven that the prayer was written in the last days of <b>Ibbi-Sin</b>, as Ur was falling.</blockquote><a href="/songs/lament-for-ur">Lament for Ur</a></li><li><blockquote>Ishbi-Erra, an official from Mari serving <b>Ibbi-Sin</b>, last king of the Neo-Sumerian Empire, made himself independent at Isin around 2017 BCE as Ur's grip failed.</blockquote><a href="/state/isin">Isin</a></li></ul></section>
